<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Bamra State</h1><p class="st-meta">1545 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Deogarh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A princely state of the Sambalpur hills in western Odisha whose 19th-century Raja made its capital Deogarh a centre of Odia printing and literature.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The ruling family claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-ganga-dynasty">Eastern Ganga dynasty</a>, and tradition dates the state to the mid-16th century. It was one of the chiefships in the orbit of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sambalpur-state">Sambalpur State</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Bamra, also called Bamanda, was a hilly, forested state. It came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhonsles">Bhonsles</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nagpur-kingdom">Nagpur Kingdom</a> and later British supervision. Raja Basudeb Sudhal Deb (r. 1869–1903) set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deogarh-odisha">Deogarh</a> that published an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odia-language">Odia</a> newspaper and books, and introduced schools and public works.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bamra merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> on 1 January 1948, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deogarh-odisha">Deogarh</a> is now the headquarters of Deogarh district.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bamra-state.jpg" alt="Bamra State" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Govern britànic de l'Índia / Government of British India · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BonaiKart_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1545 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Deogarh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Basudeb Sudhal Deb</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sambalpuri, Odia</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bamanda</dd></dl></aside></div>
